WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.100
Film Courage: How do we know if the plot progression
in a screenplay makes sense?

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.100
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: I think that it’s just common

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.100
sense.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.790
You’re going to sort of follow the logic
of your story.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.640
Is that what you mean?

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.160
Film Courage: Yes…well…maybe a better
question is what if the plot progression is

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.240
taking too long?

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.140
Maybe that’s a better question.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.440
My question is taking too long!

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.440
Sorry.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.250
Naomi: No…that’s a good question.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.070
So I think that that is subjective right?

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.910
And it depends on what you’re going for,
it depends on the story you are telling, the

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:38.930
pace that you sort of expect from the story
in that genre and that style.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:47.500
There is no black and white answer but I do
think when you’re reading a script right

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.750
one of the big red flags is boredom.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:57.710
So this might be something that you can only
figured out your entire plot and you get fresh

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:03.580
eyes on it you might think that it moves like
a runaway train and then you might have somebody

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.530
else look at it and they say “Oh gosh, when
is it going to get going?

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.490
When is it going to start?”

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:17.810
I think that the sort of natural approach
is to get your story moving as quickly as

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.279
possible and make sure you’re escalating
the conflict and escalating the stakes consistently

00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:29.819
throughout the story and building on that
throughout all of act two but if you’ve

00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:34.049
done that and broken a story, plotted the
whole thing out, you think it’s great, I

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:37.729
would still say it’s a great way to get
a fresh set of eyes on it so you can see if

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:42.079
everyone else agrees with you and they think
the pace is okay of your story as well.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:44.689
Film Courage: Right, I guess there is also
pacing that is too fast?

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:51.999
Naomi: Yes and that’s what we’re talking
about letting moments breathe because we want

00:01:51.999 --> 00:01:54.771
to sort of see the emotional reactions of
stories to.

00:01:54.771 --> 00:01:55.771
We don’t want to see the emotional reactions
of stories too.

00:01:55.771 --> 00:01:59.169
We don’t want…I mean sometimes we just
want an action movie that just movies from

00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:00.969
action-to-action right?

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:07.069
But more often I think we’re interested
in what is the effect of this plot on this

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:08.069
character.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:12.549
That’s what we actually care about when
we’re watching a movie or reading a script.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:17.310
We want to know what is the experience like
for this person going through this?

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.310
What are they feeling?

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.510
What are they thinking?

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:24.860
How is this causing them to change or re-prioritize
what is in their life?

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:31.480
So I think yes, pace in a script can be too
fast at times if it doesn’t give us those

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:37.000
moments of reaction and sort of processing
emotions so that we understand what’s the

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.220
emotional journey of this story as well not
just the plot.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:47.069
Film Courage: Going back top biopics I think
sometimes where they fall short in is the

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:51.130
pacing is too fast because they are trying
to cram too much and I know we were talking

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.590
about the movie SEBERG off camera.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.400
I thought the pacing was great but you’d
have to see it.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.340
Naomi: Biopics are tough because I think a
lot of times people try and make a story of

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.980
someone’s entire life or maybe that’s
the instinct of this was such an iconic person.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:12.019
I want to tell the whole story of their entire
life and very few peoples’ lives are actually

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:13.569
one story.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:19.230
Most of our lives are episodic so you have
to pick what is the episode from your life

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.159
that’s a transformative, defining experience,
and then tell that story.

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:24.780
Don’t tell me the whole thing.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.560
Just give me that transformative experience.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.040
Film Courage: Great point, yes.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.469
I think that’s what it did for the most
part.

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:35.959
It didn’t totally follow her whole journey.

